{
  "dom_id": "step-q61781",
  "name": "Question 6",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14249141/assets/72450d407a3c4d043769ac1e331adc1625fd8d579e5f49409cfa718116e05581.png)\n\nNash paints a box that has the form of a rectangular prism. What is the total area that Nash needs to paint if the box is $6\\,\\text{cm}$ long, $3\\,\\text{cm}$ wide, and $3\\,\\text{cm}$ tall?",
  "worked_solution": "First, let's consider the net of our box.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14249141/assets/038c0ea6632481ee1eaece1637aac46b9a09389c0d363f4b3503b1944c039127.png)\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nFrom the diagram, we have squares and rectangles.\n\nThe rectangles are $6\\,\\text{cm}$ long and $3\\,\\text{cm}$ high. So, the area of one rectangle is\n\n$$\n\\text{Area of the rectangle}=6\\times 3=18\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe squares have sides of length $3\\,\\text{cm}.$ Hence, the area of one square is\n\n$$\n\\text{Area of the square}=3\\times 3=9\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the surface area of the bar consists of $4$ rectangles and $2$ squares. \n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Surface area} & =4\\times (\\text{Area of the rectangle})+2\\times (\\text{Area of the square}) \\\\  & =4\\times 18+2\\times 9 \\\\  & =72+18 \\\\  & =90\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
